Date: Fri, 23 Apr 93 06:34:36 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: TECH: Report - Entropic Cost of Computation

Briefly, because I'm Derek will have some new perspectives after seeing
the prof:

> My unhappiness [...] involves the
> subsequent step of expressing "uncertainty of microstate" or
> (accepting equilibrium arguments) "number of microstates" in
> units of energy/temperature.

Think of k as a scaling constant, necessary to make the results of
information theory (where information/uncertainty is measured as a pure
number) applicable to the material world (where quantities tend to have
units).  Hence  H (Shannon) = k S (Gibbs) 

> why we couldn't use a base-2 log in the definition
> of S, which would seem (probably doesn't but I don't know why) to
> change the cost of bit erasure.

We can, and often do, use log-2 (rather than log-e).  The bit erasure
cost stays the same, though.  Just changes the proportionality constant
when applied to physics, but the concept of a bit if an informational
concept so the fundamental cost of erasure stays the same.

> > At equilibrium S is maximised
> 
> On this point, the text I was using defined the approach to
> equilibrium [as] the approach to SOME constant value of S; not
> necessarily the maximum

Quite right.  I was being sloppy.  Equilibria lie at local maxima of S
and on saddle points.

> > A reversible nanocomputer (or any reversible mechanism) *is* in
> > equilibrium.

Same comment as above.  The reversible mechanism has a set of states of
equal entropy, forming a local maximum.  The machine can switch from one
of these states to another at no entropic cost.

> derek

