From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: GS:  Institute of General Semantics, + Objectivism

Richard Kennaway asks:
|>        Institute of General Semantics
|>        163 Engle Street, #4B
|>        Englewood, NJ  07631
|>        (201) 568-0551
|
| Is this a separate outfit from the ISGS whose address I recently posted?
| Has there been a schism in GS?  
| Which "is" the "real" keeper of the GS flame?

Craig Presson asks:
| Is this the same as the California branch at (510) 798-0311?
| Or has GS been through schisms like Objectivism and even Loglan?


  As far as I know, the people associated with IGS and ISGS 'are' on 
good terms with each other and collaborate regularly.  I have the
impression that IGS focusses more on hardcore basic formulation issues
and training, while ISGS focusses more on popularization and application
of the principles in a variety of fields (mostly social sciences, the
humanities, and criticism of the press).  I used to subsribe to the ISGS
quarterly, _ETC_, but found that the UCSD library carries it.
  I asked similar questions when I went to the GS conference in San Diego
(circa 1985) and got the above answers as well as the lesser consideration
that since IGS 'is' on the East Coast, and ISGS on the West, sometimes
inquiries might be referred to whichever one you 'are' closer to,
but that certainly is not a firm rule.


  An additional thought:  Ever since learning of Objectivism, with its
Aristotelian orientation and its power of moral and political application,
I've been wondering if it 'is' at all possible to update it with the
scientific non-Aristotelian formulations of GS.  If that synthesis can
be accomplished, we'll have one hell of a 'conceptual' framework!

  One thing that occurs to me 'is' that since "law" involves the application
of violent force by a "government" against "its" citizens (ie, the willingness
of "law" enforcers to shoot people, if necessary, to get them to obey and
to prevent them from "resisting arrest"), that action can be modelled very
well in an Aristotelian sense.  A bullet in the head 'is' a fairly black
and white issue since it almost invariably results in the death of a person,
currently the most irreversible of processes affecting human beings.  Sure,
there 'are' some exceptions (I know of at least two people who have
survived skull-penetrating gunshot wounds), but it seems to me that a 
potential link between the two formulations can occur at that point.
It seems to me that the label "non-Aristotelian" does not necessarily
mean "anti-Aristotelian".  An Aristotelian theory might serve as a very good
first-approximation model of various human events, akin to modelling a
physical event linearly, with the understanding of course that there will
always be more information than the model accounts for.  As we learn more,
we can alter the model with non-A considerations, akin to using nonlinear
mathematical descriptions of physical processes, while keeping the A model
as either a limiting case, reference point, or practical working model for
crude calculations.

  I haven't fully thought out the matter (obviously), but now and then I
find it an interesting notion ...

