     Sorry for sending a blank message to the list.  I was not paying
close enough attention to what my mail software was doing.

     Thanks for the clarification of your hypothesis.  As I
thought, it answered the questions I had raised, and I find I
cannot argue with it as it applies to human history--up to
very recent times.  In responding, I have been forced to give
my ideas more form and structure than they had before this
discussion started.  I have not been posting for long, and
this is an unexpected benefit.  Hopefully, I will be as clear
in stating where I diverge from your hypothesis as you were
in stating it. I hope it doesn't sound too pedantic.
     
     Perhaps because I am a physics student, the first
analogy to come to mind is the comparison of Newtonian
mechanics and special relativity.  Newtonian mechanics is
applicable to all our everyday experience, including aircraft
and any rocket we can build now; and special relativity--as
it must--gives the same answers with a negligible divergence
to the same phenomena.  But their predictions diverge
significantly as velocities increase. The gamma in my
hypothesis is computing power.  As I understand what you're
saying, there is something like a one to one function between
population and progress.  If I = rate of invention, and P =
population, then:

     r(p = I*P where I is constant;

my contention is that:

     r(p = I*P*C

where C is computing power.  Through just about all of human
history, C was effectively one--the computing power of the
human brain is P.  So my function gives the same answers as
yours.  Accelerating population growth yields accelerating
progress.  Even for the first few decades after its
invention, C was pretty close to one plus negligible--but not
now.  (Actually, my first thought was: r(p = I*(P+C); but I
think that the human/machine combination is more than an
addition of capabilities.  As the interface between the two
improves, the relationship between C and P will become more
synergistic.  Even if it is more like this function, the
divergence would be smaller, but I think still significant by
now.)  I will try to get some hard numbers if you want them,
but I intuit that C is still much smaller than P, but is
growing faster.  The physics analogy breaks down here because
Newtonian mechanics gives values that are too high as
velocity approaches speed of light, whereas your hypothesis
gives values that are too low as we approach singularity.

     After thinking a little more, and without erasing any of
the above, I think the relationship is more complicated. 
Since I contend that the rate of invention is a function of
the computer/human interaction, r(p is a composite function:

     r(p = r(I = C*P

     which still leaves your hypothesis intact up to I'd say
the last 20 years.

     So I would modify my original statement about the
utility of your theory having been swamped by our modern
ability to communicate and process information; instead, I
would say that it will be in thirty years.  

     What do you think?  Do you think I'm wasting both our
time with this line of thought?  I'm enjoying it anyway. 
It's too bad nobody else is interested in this discussion. 
I'm sure someone on the list could work up a better function
than this. 

