Date: Sat, 24 Apr 1993 04:34:15 -0500 (EDT)
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: Speed of Light Limitation

Mark Read Pickens writes:
> 
> 
> Can someone tell me the origin of the idea that the speed of light is the
> limit to motion in the universe?  I realize mass is supposed to increase
> with velocity, theoretically reaching infinity at light-speed.  Since this
> would be impossible, said mass is necessarily restricted to sub-light
> speeds.
>  
> Why was this assumption made and what kind of research substantiates it?  

   The idea follows from the assumption that the laws of physics
are the same for all inertial observers -- those moving at constant
velocities with respect to each other. This idea dates back to
Galileo, but Einstein generalized it to all the laws of physics.

  Another assumption is that there is an ultimate speed, c, which is the
same for all inertial observers (that is, no matter what speed you
are traveling,  the measured speed c will be the same) Light, being
special, happens to travel at this ultimate speed. Using Maxwell's
laws to derive the speed of electromagnetic waves, one sees that
their speed depends only on two constants, the permittivity constant,
and the permeabilty constant. These constants are intrinsic to space and
don't change, so the speed of light is constant in free space.

  Neither of these assumptions are unreasonable. If postulate #1
didn't hold, you could perform an experiment on a train which would
yield different results if performed 'stationary'. 

  The speed of light postulate might be a little harder to swallow, but
hundreds of experiments over decades lend proof to its correctness.

 The consequences of not accepting these postulates lead to conclusions
that have been ruled out by experiment.  Consider the intuitive
rule for adding velocities (v' = v + v_frame). If you were standing on
a train and turned on a flash light, from my standpoint (stationary),
the light pulse would travel at v_train + c (according to the old rule).
But this contradicts postulate number 2, so we have two choices.
1) Either the old-rule for adding velocities is false (against our intuition)
2) the laws of electricity and magnetism are different depending on your
inertial frame.

  Accepting #2 means that there is a 'preferred' frame in which the speed of
light actually does travel at c.  Michelson-Morley performed an
experiment to test this hypothesis and it failed. 

  As to why things like gravity waves or neutrinos couldn't travel
faster than light or why light travels at the "ultimate speed" and not 
a little slower, I'm not sure right now, but it's probably related to
electromagnetism not working correctly if they true. (and since
we know our electromagneticism laws work, we don't want to discard them)

  One of the physics Czars like Robin Hanson or Mike Price will probably
give a better answer.


